WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:11.100
So, on that note we now turn to that that which makes everything possible

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.400
which is the financial report from you, Paul.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.900
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: So no expectations there then and

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.180
of course, the profession
which people really trust is not nursing or

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:34.280
indeed medical people, but accountants, because
we all need them he said. The

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:40.399
report in front of you sets out the latest
financial performance data for the commissioning

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:44.760
system, and our forecast for the remainder
of the year. As this meeting is quite a bit

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:50.420
earlier in the month than our normal pattern,
we do not yet have the data for November,

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.829
which will be coming through actually next
week, just before I go off for a merry Christmas

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:59.730
break, so this reflects the position as it
was at the end of October.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:05.030
There is considerable continuity between what
I am about to take you through and the report

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:10.110
that I brought last time we met, back in September,
and indeed with the half-year numbers that

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:15.220
we published while we did not have a Board
meeting last month, in three key respects

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:19.720
of continuity. Firstly, the core forecast
that you can see for the commissioning sector

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.600
as a whole remains at break-even; a marginal
overspend of £7 million, which is well within

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.380
the roundings on a budget of £106 billion.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.540
Secondly, the report again highlights the
reasons why this year is bringing unprecedented

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:40.240
challenges to CCGs in particular, within an
overall settlement which is by any standards

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:46.010
exceptionally tight, in terms of real purchasing
power, once you strip out population growth,

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:51.079
inflation and the very significant changes
to pension charges that we had to cope with

00:01:51.079 --> 00:01:57.570
in 2016/2017. In that context, the need to
create a risk reserve of £800 million to

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:03.780
cover off risks within both the commissioning
position and the provider sectors has substantially

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:09.350
increased the level of commissioner efficiencies
required from £1.9 billion of efficiency

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:16.360
achieved last year to £3.1 billion that we
have set out in this year's plans, with most

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:21.580
of that increase coming in the CCG sector.
To state the blindingly obvious, this is a

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:26.780
big ask, and it is great that most CCGs have
risen brilliantly to the challenge of doing

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:32.790
that, and many of them are reporting efficiency
gains well in excess of previous years. If

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:40.260
you want a flavour for that, just about 80%
of CCGs will be delivering more than 2% of

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.819
commissioner efficiencies this year compared
to less than half of them last year, so there

00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:52.860
is a marked step-up in the delivery of efficiencies
by the CCG sector in particular. It is not

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.830
really surprising that a number of CCGs are
struggling to deliver that ambition in full,

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.900
leading to a level of risk in their positions
which has been pretty consistent, at just

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:07.450
over £400 million, since the very first forecast
for the year, and which is progressively translating

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:13.300
into overspends recognised in the forecast.
That is the second similarity.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.709
The third one is that, despite the continuing
challenges in the CCG sector, the net risk

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:23.600
position across the whole of the NHS England
Group has continued to reduce as a consequence

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.830
of the mitigating actions that I highlighted
back in September, particularly related to

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:34.840
the systematic identification and then release
to the bottom line of underspends, contingencies

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.830
and reserves in our central budgets. As a
consequence, there has been a progressive

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:47.050
improvement in the net risk position across
the Group, from £454 million as at the end

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:53.050
of the first quarter to the £373 million
I reported at our September meeting, the £280

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:58.299
million included in our half-year report,
and now the £215 million that you can see

00:03:58.299 --> 00:04:03.810
as at month seven. I would just counsel you
strongly against linear extrapolation of that

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:09.299
trend; delightful as it would be, and confirming
as it would be of my golden month coinage

00:04:09.299 --> 00:04:13.470
earlier on, that would be a very unwise assumption
to make, but at least we can see that over

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:19.390
previous months we have been bringing down
the net risk across the commissioning sector.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:24.150
It does indicate that we are delivering on
our commitment to take all possible actions

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:29.280
to secure a result as close as possible to
break-even by the end of the year. All of

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.770
those numbers, of course, are before allowing
for the £800 million reserve I mentioned

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:39.550
earlier. That reserve remains intact, and
decisions about its use, as between non-recurrent

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:43.970
investment and release to the bottom line
to offset the impact of deficits across the

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:51.400
sector, will be taken early in 2017 in the
light of third-quarter results. So much for

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.000
the general introduction to the numbers.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.250
Appendix A shows you some more detail on the
broadly balanced core forecast position that

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:05.130
I have just indicated. The CCG challenges
are reflected right across the country in

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:13.449
year-to-date overspends averaging 0.7%, and
affecting 93 CCGs, up from 77 at month five.

00:05:13.449 --> 00:05:18.780
Roughly half of those organisations are currently
forecasting to recover that position by the

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.440
end of the year, and you can see that that
expected recovery is strongest in the Midlands

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.000
and East, which has a particularly strong
process for intervention where CCGs start

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.700
to go off track, and has been leading the
work across the country on strengthening QIPP,

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:38.121
or commissioner efficiency delivery. However,
as I mentioned earlier, the recovery in the

00:05:38.121 --> 00:05:42.200
Midlands and East, and elsewhere, is not without
risk, and I do anticipate that we will see

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:48.080
further adverse movement in the CCG forecast
as the risks highlighted elsewhere in my report

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.280
continue to crystallise.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:54.850
You can see that specialised commissioning
is again expecting to balance its books by

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:59.889
the end of the year, despite significant ongoing
challenges, particularly in the South region.

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:04.210
We do have high levels of confidence in that
forecast, partly because of the greatly improved

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:08.800
controls that we have put in place around
specialised commissioning, and partly because

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.910
some of the cost risks that we anticipated
in central specialised commissioning budgets

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:18.569
have not, at least so far, materialised to
the extent that we expected.

00:06:18.569 --> 00:06:21.730
As you can see, there are small underspends
forecast in the other direct commissioning

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:26.060
budgets. Given the particular focus we have
on the challenges and opportunities in GP

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:30.710
services, it is perhaps worth just saying
that spending on that area within primary

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:35.950
care is broadly in line with the significantly
increased budget that we set for this year.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:40.690
I have mentioned already the underspends we
are actively managing in central budgets.

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:45.219
That has been helped by some one-off issues,
or one-off opportunities, particularly around

00:06:45.219 --> 00:06:50.240
rates rebates, but it is mainly a question
of freezing reserves and capturing underspends

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:54.370
as they arise, in order to contribute to the
bottom-line position while protecting the

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:59.950
spend on front-line services. Even after allowing
for the oddities of Treasury accounting - and

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:04.009
I am afraid there are a few, particularly
in relation to provisions, which are reflected

00:07:04.009 --> 00:07:07.319
in the adjustments towards the bottom of the
table - this would give us a clear line of

00:07:07.319 --> 00:07:11.059
sight to what is effectively a balanced position.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:15.889
I think I have already covered off the very
real risks highlighted in paragraphs 13 to

00:07:15.889 --> 00:07:20.390
15 of my report. Having completed the intensive
review with regional colleagues which I mentioned

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:24.910
last time, I can give the Board full assurance
that everything possible is being done, by

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:29.630
CCGs themselves and by our teams up and down
the country working with them, to minimise

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:34.400
and mitigate these risks. I think it is reasonable
to assume that our range of likely delivery

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.669
lies between the break-even forecast that
I have presented to you and the round-about

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:45.039
£220 million risk-adjusted overspend position
indicated in the risk part of the report.

00:07:45.039 --> 00:07:49.830
When you combine that range with the £800
million reserve I mentioned at the start,

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:54.120
that gives us the potential to contribute
a substantial net underspend to the overall

00:07:54.120 --> 00:08:01.240
NHS position, should that be required to offset
a deficit in the provider sector. I have to

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:05.490
say that, whilst final decisions have yet
to be made on that score, it seems highly

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:10.949
likely that the bulk of that underspend will
be needed in the light of a planned provider

00:08:10.949 --> 00:08:16.479
deficit of £580 million, and a half year
forecast by NHS Improvement, after allowing

00:08:16.479 --> 00:08:24.800
for full deployment of the £1.8 billion sustainability
funding, of £669 million. So far at least,

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:29.450
the actions announced in the so-called Reset
Document earlier in the year, and in particular

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:33.750
the initiative to bear down on what had been
rapidly growing agency spend and the work

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:38.020
with the first two cohorts of trusts in special
measures, have helped to contain the risks

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:42.830
in the provider sector, but they have not
had the impact of reducing the deficit towards

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:48.230
the £250 million which NHSI signalled as
its ambition for the year.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:53.700
I mentioned last time that speculation about
additional funding for the NHS was misplaced,

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:58.190
and the Autumn Statement has of course reconfirmed
that. The Board is also aware, I know, that

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:02.010
the department has already committed all of
its available resources to the allocation

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:08.580
provided in the mandate, so the NHS very much
needs to stand on its own two feet this year.

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:11.930
I can assure the Board that we are playing
our full part in that, and I would want to

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:16.550
put on record really, as my conclusion of
this report, the enormous commitment and admirable

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:21.160
resilience which commissioning organisations
across the country are demonstrating in tackling

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:25.350
simultaneously the massive operational and
financial challenges that we have been setting

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:30.670
out in the last two reports, in a year that
was always going to be exceptionally testing

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:36.170
and is very much living up to our expectations
in that respect; and at the same time, engaging

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:41.050
with the very exciting but no less challenging
agenda of planning long, medium and short

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:46.750
term for sustainability and transformation,
which we discussed earlier in the agenda.

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:51.100
On which note, I commend the report to the
Board, and as always, look forward to your

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.760
contributions on any of the topics that I
have covered.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:10:00.420
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Paul, thank you very much for that report.  Let me invite contributions and comments

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:12.080
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: I suppose I would just like
to endorse Paul's final comment, and recognise

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.500
the efforts that are going on in clinical
commissioning groups that are contributing

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:19.620
to these numbers, which look not as frightening
as they might.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.820
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think we would all concur
with that, whilst understanding that this

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:29.440
is held together, and is quite a fragile situation
that we find ourselves in. I would add also

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:34.860
a commendation to Paul and his team; I think
it is an extraordinary amount of work that

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:40.520
results in such clarity of exposition, but
behind that is a huge complexity of operation,

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:42.090
so thank you, Paul.

